// kyoten::frame::TextEntry — whether a player is typing (the first slice's step 8, its fifth part, KYR-54;
// design/input.md § Decided, 2026-11-25, questions 2 to 4). It is Output's own state, `"output": true` in the frame
// tier's records: an Output processor turns it on when the game wants the player to type, a UI field taking focus or the
// game's own Output processor reading its simulation's state, and no other phase reads it, since it changes with the
// frame rate. A game's prefab lists it on whatever entity draws the field: `"TextEntry": { "player": 1 }`.
//
// The platform reads every one after each frame:
// - while one naming a player is on, that player's `Text` actions collect what they type, and the window turns on its
//   text input, the IME and an on-screen keyboard where there is no physical one (`@kama/window`'s WIN-5);
// - meanwhile the player's keyboard drives only their `Text` actions and the entries marked `"typing": true`.
//
// The field's purpose (text, a number, an email, a password), its longest length, its lines and its box on screen join
// it with WIN-5, when a platform can use them.

export { TextEntry };

@generate(Serializable, Deserializable)
type value TextEntry {
    // The player it is for, numbered from 1; 0 names none, and turns nothing on.
    @field public int32 player = 0;
    // Whether that player is typing.
    @field public bool on = false;

    // Naming no player, and off.
    public default ctor make() { }
}
